\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{Arrows of selected backgrounds}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{The groupoid #{\mathsf{Bg}} has \vocabk{selected backgrounds}{fdip-005A}
as objects. An arrow #{b\to b'} is a pair of bijections
#{p:V\to V'}, #{q:E\to E'} satisfying
##{\begin{aligned}
s'q&=ps,&h'q&=ph,\\
w'q&=w,&m'&=m,\\
P'&=UPU^{-1},&&(U\phi)(p(v))=\phi(v).
\end{aligned}}
Here #{U:H\to H'} is the vertex counting isometry, distinct
from the selector #{P}. For coordinate selectors the last equation
is equivalent to #{pR=R'}, so these are precisely the
\vocabk{exact relabelings}{fdip-000G}.}

\p{The larger groupoid #{\mathsf{Bg}^{\pm}} allows
\vocabk{signed endpoint arrows}{fdip-003B}: an additional sign
#{\varepsilon(e)\in\{+,-\}} specifies whether #{q(e)} has
endpoints #{(ps(e),ph(e))} or #{(ph(e),ps(e))}.
Weights, mass and the selector obey the same equations.
The signed edge map is
#{(Ja)(q(e))=\varepsilon(e)a(e)}, where the sign acts as #{\pm1}.
Then #{d'U=Jd} and #{W'J=JW}. Consequently
##{Q'=UQU^{-1},\qquad G'=UGU^{-1}.}
The endpoint condition is retained even for loops of zero incidence.
Signs are part of an arrow, not merely an equation between Laplacians.}

\p{Compose vertex and edge bijections, and for signed arrows use
##{\varepsilon_{21}(e)=\varepsilon_2(q_1(e))\varepsilon_1(e).}
The identity has identity bijections and positive signs; the inverse
has inverse bijections and sign #{\varepsilon(e)} at #{q(e)}.
The \vocabk{endpoint-arrow proof}{fdip-003B} checks all graph equations.
Selector transport also composes because
#{P''=U_2U_1P(U_2U_1)^{-1}}. It inverts by conjugating back.
These operations therefore give groupoids on the stated tuples.}

\p{Restrict #{U} to the isometry #{U_X:X\to X'} of selected spaces.
Then the [selected laws](fdip-005B) satisfy
##{\begin{aligned}
C_{P'}&=U_XC_PU_X^{-1},\\
S_{P'}&=U_XS_PU_X^{-1},\\
(U_X)_*\nu_b&=\nu_{b'}.
\end{aligned}}
For the first identity insert #{P'=UPU^{-1}} and #{G'=UGU^{-1}};
inversion gives the second. The [full-law transport](fdip-000H)
gives #{U_*\mu_Q=\mu_{Q'}}, while #{P'U=U_XP}. Composition
of Borel pushforwards proves the third. In particular, for an
integrable retained observable #{F'} on #{X'},
##{\int_{X'}F'\,\extd\nu_{b'}=\int_X F'\circ U_X\,\extd\nu_b.}
The equality for #{|F'|}, allowing infinity, first establishes
the matching integrability condition.}

\p{These arrows carry incidence, not only the precision and projector.
Replacing one edge of weight #{w} by two parallel edges with the
same endpoints and positive weights summing to #{w} leaves #{Q}
unchanged, but changes #{|E|}; it admits no arrow in either groupoid.
Reversing the orientation of a specified edge gives an identity-on-vertices
signed arrow with negative sign on that edge. That particular
correspondence is not directed. Another directed isomorphism may exist
if the graph has additional symmetries.}
